EXT. DRY SALT FLAT - ABANDONED STAGECOACH CROSSING - DAY

White ground in every direction. A cracked water trough, a hitching post, and one leaning shed. CALEB DRUMMOND (40s, sunburned, dusty duster) sits on an upturned bucket, counting out coins onto a flat stone. His mule DOLLY stands hipshot beside him, one ear back.

                    CALEB
          Eleven dollars and a button.

He lifts the button and flicks it away.

                    CALEB (CONT'D)
          (to the mule)
          You eat more than a church.

He listens. Wind. Nothing else. He gets up, walks to the trough, and looks into it.

                    CALEB (CONT'D)
          (to himself)
          Dry as a Baptist's Sunday. Should've gone north.

He cups his hands to his mouth and calls out across the flat.

                    CALEB (CONT'D)
          Anybody in that shed, I got a rifle and no patience!

The shed door creaks. A figure leans in the shadow. Caleb levels the rifle.

OLD MARISOL VEGA (70s, black rebozo, a cane carved with snakes) steps into the light, squinting. She carries a tin cup.

                    MARISOL
          You will scare the water, shouting like that.

                    CALEB
          There's no water.

                    MARISOL
          Under the third board of the shed. Two feet down. It comes back at noon.

Caleb lowers the rifle slowly.

                    CALEB
          Ma'am, I about shot you.

                    MARISOL
          Many have wanted to.

She offers him the tin cup. He takes it, turns it over in his hand, then looks at the sun.

                    CALEB
          (muttering)
          Two feet. Third board. Lord help me.

He tips his hat to her, takes the shovel from the wall, and heads for the shed. Dolly follows, flicking her tail.

                    MARISOL
          (calling after him)
          Share with the animal. She has been patient.

                    CALEB
          (without turning)
          Always am, ma'am. Always am.
